WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.042
Lesson 5 is one of the richest in the module because it gathers several earlier ideas into one bigger whole.

2
00:00:07.042 --> 00:00:14.084
Temperature gave us an average motion clue, but internal energy asks for a whole-system total.

3
00:00:14.084 --> 00:00:21.126
It includes the total kinetic energy of all particles and the total potential energy linked to their arrangement and attractions.

4
00:00:21.126 --> 00:00:28.168
That means internal energy can change because the crowd is larger, because the state arrangement is different, or because both factors have changed.

5
00:00:28.168 --> 00:00:35.210
The worked example becomes much more logical when students stop looking for one single meter reading and instead build the store out of its parts.

6
00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:42.252
Keep this takeaway in view: Internal energy starts to make sense when students build one store from two contributions instead of confusing it with temperature.

7
00:00:42.252 --> 00:00:49.294
Whole-system idea means internal energy.

8
00:00:49.294 --> 00:00:56.336
This quantity belongs to the sample as a whole, not to one particle.

9
00:00:56.336 --> 00:01:03.378
Part one means total kinetic.

10
00:01:03.378 --> 00:01:10.420
All particle motion contributes to the whole-system store.

11
00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:17.064
This diagram is important because it makes internal energy feel like a real combined store.

12
00:01:17.064 --> 00:01:23.709
One part comes from the motion of all the particles.

13
00:01:23.709 --> 00:01:30.353
Another part comes from the way the particles are arranged and held together.

14
00:01:30.353 --> 00:01:36.998
Change the sample size and the total can rise.

15
00:01:36.998 --> 00:01:43.642
Change the state or arrangement while keeping the temperature the same and the total can still shift.

16
00:01:43.642 --> 00:01:50.287
The picture matters because it shows why internal energy cannot be reduced to one average reading like temperature.

17
00:01:50.287 --> 00:01:56.931
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong internal-energy visual keeps both the motion part and the arrangement part on the same ledger.

18
00:01:56.931 --> 00:02:03.576
Motion share: The whole crowd's kinetic contribution depends on the motion of all particles together.

19
00:02:03.576 --> 00:02:10.220
Arrangement share: The particle-link or potential contribution depends on how the particles are organized and attracted.

20
00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:16.332
A useful analogy is a city budget made from two major parts: the money flowing through people's activity and the money locked into buildings and infrastructure.

21
00:02:16.332 --> 00:02:22.444
Two cities could share one average indicator yet still have very different total budgets because the population or infrastructure picture differs.

22
00:02:22.444 --> 00:02:28.555
Internal energy behaves in a similar way.

23
00:02:28.555 --> 00:02:34.667
It is not one narrow reading.

24
00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:40.779
It is a whole-system total built from motion and arrangement together.

25
00:02:40.779 --> 00:02:46.891
The analogy helps students understand why same temperature does not settle the entire store story.

26
00:02:46.891 --> 00:02:53.003
Keep this takeaway in view: The city-budget analogy helps the learner accept internal energy as a combined total with more than one source.

27
00:02:53.003 --> 00:02:59.115
Activity share means motion part.

28
00:02:59.115 --> 00:03:05.226
One part of the total comes from what is moving in the system.

29
00:03:05.226 --> 00:03:11.338
Structure share means arrangement part.

30
00:03:11.338 --> 00:03:17.450
Another part comes from how the system is built and linked.

31
00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:23.819
The core definition is that internal energy equals the total kinetic energy plus the total potential energy of the particles.

32
00:03:23.819 --> 00:03:30.188
Each word matters.

33
00:03:30.188 --> 00:03:36.558
Total means the whole sample.

34
00:03:36.558 --> 00:03:42.927
Kinetic means motion.

35
00:03:42.927 --> 00:03:49.296
Potential means arrangement and attraction.

36
00:03:49.296 --> 00:03:55.665
This definition is what allows the lesson to explain why two samples with the same temperature can still have different internal energies.

37
00:03:55.665 --> 00:04:02.035
The average kinetic-energy clue from temperature is not the same as the whole-system total of kinetic plus potential contributions.

38
00:04:02.035 --> 00:04:08.404
Keep this takeaway in view: The definition becomes usable once students hear it as a whole-sample ledger rather than as a fancy synonym for temperature.

39
00:04:08.404 --> 00:04:14.773
If crowd size grows: The total internal energy can grow even when the temperature is unchanged.

40
00:04:14.773 --> 00:04:21.142
If state changes: The potential-energy part can change because the arrangement and attractions change.

41
00:04:21.142 --> 00:04:27.512
internal energy = total kinetic energy + total potential energy of particles.

42
00:04:27.512 --> 00:04:33.881
temperature gives an average kinetic clue, not the whole total.

43
00:04:33.881 --> 00:04:40.250
The word total is the protection against collapsing internal energy into a one-particle or one-average idea.

44
00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:45.913
A good routine is to ask what changed between the cases.

45
00:04:45.913 --> 00:04:51.575
Did the number of particles change?

46
00:04:51.575 --> 00:04:57.238
Did the state or arrangement change?

47
00:04:57.238 --> 00:05:02.901
Did the average motion change as well?

48
00:05:02.901 --> 00:05:08.564
Internal-energy questions become much more manageable when the student treats them like store-comparison questions rather than temperature-guessing questions.

49
00:05:08.564 --> 00:05:14.226
The learner can then decide whether the kinetic part, the potential part, or both might have shifted.

50
00:05:14.226 --> 00:05:19.889
Keep this takeaway in view: The store-comparison routine helps students see internal energy as something that can change for more than one reason.

51
00:05:19.889 --> 00:05:25.552
Step 1 means check sample size.

52
00:05:25.552 --> 00:05:31.215
More particles can raise the total even if the temperature matches.

53
00:05:31.215 --> 00:05:36.877
Step 2 means check arrangement.

54
00:05:36.877 --> 00:05:42.540
A different state can change the potential-energy share.

55
00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:46.381
Run four checks.

56
00:05:46.381 --> 00:05:50.221
Does internal energy belong to one particle or the whole sample?

57
00:05:50.221 --> 00:05:54.062
The whole sample.

58
00:05:54.062 --> 00:05:57.903
Does it include only kinetic energy?

59
00:05:57.903 --> 00:06:01.743
No, it includes kinetic and potential parts.

60
00:06:01.743 --> 00:06:05.584
If two samples have the same temperature, must their internal energies match?

61
00:06:05.584 --> 00:06:09.425
No.

62
00:06:09.425 --> 00:06:13.265
Can changing the state affect the internal-energy story even when the temperature clue is not enough by itself?

63
00:06:13.265 --> 00:06:17.106
Yes.

64
00:06:17.106 --> 00:06:20.947
Those are the exact ideas the worked example will rely on.

65
00:06:20.947 --> 00:06:24.787
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can treat internal energy as a rich whole-system total rather than a thin temperature label.

66
00:06:24.787 --> 00:06:28.628
Ownership means whole sample.

67
00:06:28.628 --> 00:06:32.469
Internal energy is a whole-system quantity.

68
00:06:32.469 --> 00:06:36.309
Composition means KE + PE.

69
00:06:36.309 --> 00:06:40.150
Motion and arrangement both contribute.

70
00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:46.984
You are ready for the worked example now.

71
00:06:46.984 --> 00:06:53.817
Start by stating the full definition of internal energy as total kinetic plus total potential energy of the particles.

72
00:06:53.817 --> 00:07:00.651
Then ask what changed between the cases: sample size, state arrangement, average motion, or some combination.

73
00:07:00.651 --> 00:07:07.485
Do not let temperature answer more than it is allowed to answer.

74
00:07:07.485 --> 00:07:14.318
The strongest solutions in this lesson explain which part of the total-store story has changed and why.

75
00:07:14.318 --> 00:07:21.152
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the learner can now use the internal-energy definition actively rather than reciting it passively.

76
00:07:21.152 --> 00:07:27.985
Definition move: Bring the full whole-system meaning into the answer before comparing the cases.

77
00:07:27.985 --> 00:07:34.819
Comparison move: Separate size changes from arrangement changes instead of blending them immediately.

78
00:07:34.819 --> 00:07:41.653
state the full internal-energy definition.

79
00:07:41.653 --> 00:07:48.486
check sample size, arrangement, and average-motion clues separately.

80
00:07:48.486 --> 00:07:55.320
The best answers use the definition as a reasoning tool, not just as a memory line.
